.TH FIND 1 2026-10-20 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
find \- search for files in a directory hierarchy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B find
.RB [ \-HLP ]
.RI [ path ...]
.RI [ expression ]
.br
.B find \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B find
searches the directory tree below each
.I path
for files that satisfy an
.IR expression ,
and acts on them.
This
.B find
is the busybox applet; see
.BR busybox (1).
.PP
The operands before the first one that begins with
.BR \- ,
or is
.B !\&
or
.BR ( ,
are the paths, and the rest is the expression.
A path cannot begin with
.BR \- ;
write
.B ./\-name
instead.
With no
.IR path ,
.B find
searches the current directory.
A
.I path
that is not a directory is tested by itself.
.PP
For each file, the expression is true or false.
Actions in it can print the file's name, run a command, or delete the file.
When the expression contains none of
.BR \-print ,
.BR \-print0 ,
.BR \-exec ,
.BR \-ok ,
or
.BR \-delete ,
.B find
adds a
.B \-print
for the whole expression, so it prints the name of each file for which the
expression is true.
With no expression, it prints every file.
.PP
Names are written on standard output, one per line, each ending in LF.
The name of a path is as given.
The name of an entry in a directory is the directory's name, a
.BR / ,
and the entry's name, with no second
.B /
when the directory's name already ends in one, and none after a bare
drive such as
.BR c: .
.PP
The expression is checked before the search starts.
An unrecognized argument, or a primary with a missing or invalid argument,
is reported on standard error, and the exit status is 1.
A file that cannot be examined, or a directory that cannot be opened, is
reported on standard error, and the search goes on.
.SS Traversal
.B find
searches the paths in the order given.
It tests a directory before the entries in it, unless
.B \-depth
or
.B \-delete
says otherwise.
The entries of a directory come in the order the file system lists them;
.B find
does not sort them.
The entries
.B .\&
and
.B ..\&
are never visited.
.PP
A symbolic link is tested as itself and not entered, unless
.BR \-H ,
.BR \-L ,
or
.B \-follow
says to follow it.
.B find
does not detect loops.
.SS Operators
Operators combine tests and actions.
They are listed from the highest precedence to the lowest.
.TP
\fB(\fR \fIexpression\fR \fB)\fR
Group.
Each parenthesis is a separate argument, and the shell needs both quoted.
.TP
\fB!\fR, \fB\-not\fR \fIexpression\fR
True when
.I expression
is false.
Negations may be stacked.
.TP
\fIexpr1\fR [\fB\-a\fR | \fB\-and\fR] \fIexpr2\fR
True when both are true.
If
.I expr1
is false,
.I expr2
is not evaluated.
Two expressions side by side mean this.
.TP
\fIexpr1\fR \fB\-o\fR | \fB\-or\fR \fIexpr2\fR
True when either is true.
If
.I expr1
is true,
.I expr2
is not evaluated.
.PP
There is no
.B ,
operator.
.SS Global options
These apply to the whole search wherever they appear in the expression.
They are not tests, so put them before the tests.
A
.B !\&
before one applies to the next test instead.
An alternative of
.B \-o
that holds only global options is empty, and an empty alternative ends
the expression:
.B "\-name a \-o \-xdev \-o \-name b"
never tests
.BR "\-name b" ,
and
.B "\-xdev \-o \-name b"
is true for every file.
The argument of
.B \-maxdepth
or
.B \-mindepth
is a decimal integer from 0 to 2147483647.
.TP
.B \-follow
The same as
.BR \-L ,
but may appear in the expression.
.TP
.B \-xdev
Enter a directory only if it is on the same volume as one of the paths.
Volumes are told apart by serial number, and a directory whose volume
busybox cannot read counts as another volume.
A directory on another volume is still tested.
With
.BR \-L ,
this keeps
.B find
from crossing a junction or symbolic link to another volume.
.TP
.BI "\-maxdepth " n
Do not descend below level
.IR n .
A directory at level
.I n
is tested but not entered.
.B "\-maxdepth 0"
tests only the paths.
.TP
.BI "\-mindepth " n
Do not test files fewer than
.I n
levels below the paths.
A path is at level 0, and the entries directly in it are at level 1.
.TP
.B \-depth
Test the entries in a directory before the directory itself.
.B \-prune
then has no effect.
.SS Tests
Where a test takes a number
.IR n ,
.BI + n
means more than
.IR n ,
.BI \- n
means less than
.IR n ,
and a plain
.I n
means exactly
.IR n .
Numbers are decimal.
.TP
.BI "\-name " pattern
The last component of the path matches the shell
.IR pattern .
The last component is the part after the last
.BR / ,
.BR \e ,
or
.BR : .
When a path ends in one of these, trailing slashes are dropped and only a
.B /
counts, so
.B a/dir/
is tested as
.BR dir ,
and
.BR c:src/ ,
.BR src\e ,
and
.B c:
as
.BR c:src ,
.BR src\e ,
and
.BR c: .
In the pattern,
.B *
matches any string,
.B ?\&
matches any one byte, and
.B [
starts a set of bytes, as in
.BR [a\-f] ,
.BR [!0\-9] ,
or
.BR [[:alpha:]] ,
with
.B !\&
or
.B ^
first for the complement.
A backslash quotes the next character.
The match is case-sensitive, and a leading period is not special.
.TP
.BI "\-iname " pattern
Like
.BR \-name ,
but ASCII letters match in either case.
.TP
\fB\-path\fR, \fB\-wholename\fR \fIpattern\fR
The whole path, as printed, matches
.IR pattern .
Here
.B *
and
.B ?\&
also match
.BR / .
.TP
.BI "\-ipath " pattern
Like
.BR \-path ,
but ASCII letters match in either case.
.TP
.BI "\-regex " pattern
The whole path, as printed, matches the POSIX basic regular expression
.IR pattern ,
which may use the GNU operators
.BR \e| ,
.BR \e+ ,
and
.BR \e? .
The match is case-sensitive.
An invalid expression is an error.
.TP
.BI "\-type " c
The file is of type
.IR c :
.B f
for a regular file,
.B d
for a directory,
.B l
for a symbolic link,
.B c
for a character device,
.B b
for a block device,
.B p
for a named pipe, or
.B s
for a socket.
See
.B NOTES
for what these are on Windows.
.TP
.B \-executable
The file is executable: a directory, a file whose name ends in
.BR .com ,
.BR .exe ,
.BR .sh ,
.BR .bat ,
or
.BR .cmd ,
a file that begins with
.BR #! ,
or a Windows program.
.TP
.BI "\-perm " mode
The file's permission bits equal
.IR mode ,
which is octal, up to 7777, or symbolic as for
.BR chmod ,
starting from no permissions.
With a leading
.BR \- ,
all the bits of
.I mode
must be set, and with a leading
.B /
or
.BR + ,
at least one.
.TP
.BI "\-mtime " n
The file's data was last modified
.I n
days ago.
The age is the time since then in whole days of 24 hours, with any
remainder dropped.
So
.B \-mtime 0
is an age under 1 day,
.B \-mtime 1
is from 1 day to under 2, and
.B \-mtime +1
is 2 days or more.
.TP
.BI "\-atime " n
Like
.BR \-mtime ,
for the time of last access.
.TP
.BI "\-ctime " n
Like
.BR \-mtime ,
for the time of creation.
.TP
.BI "\-mmin " n
Like
.BR \-mtime ,
in minutes.
.TP
.BI "\-amin " n
Like
.BR \-atime ,
in minutes.
.TP
.BI "\-cmin " n
Like
.BR \-ctime ,
in minutes.
.TP
.BI "\-newer " file
The file was modified later than
.IR file ,
comparing whole seconds.
.I file
is examined once, before the search starts, and a link is followed.
If it cannot be examined, that is an error.
.TP
.BI "\-inum " n
The file's index on its volume is
.IR n .
.TP
.BI "\-samefile " file
The file has the same volume and index as
.IR file :
it is
.IR file ,
or another name for it.
.I file
is examined before the search starts, and a link is not followed unless
.BR \-L ,
.BR \-H ,
or an earlier
.B \-follow
says to.
.TP
\fB\-size \fIn\fR[\fBcwbk\fR]
The file's length is
.I n
units.
The unit is
.B c
for 1 byte,
.B w
for 2,
.B b
for 512, or
.B k
for 1024, and is
.B b
when omitted.
The length is compared exactly, not rounded up to whole units, so
.B "\-size 1k"
matches only a file of 1024 bytes.
.TP
.BI "\-links " n
The file has
.I n
links.
.TP
.B \-empty
The file is a regular file of length 0, or a directory with no entries.
A directory that cannot be opened is reported on standard error and is
not empty, and the exit status is not affected.
.SS Actions
.TP
.B \-prune
Always true.
If the file is a directory, do not enter it.
.TP
.B \-print
Print the file's name.
Always true.
.TP
.B \-print0
Print the file's name followed by a NUL instead of a newline.
Always true.
.TP
\fB\-exec \fIcommand\fR [\fIargument\fR ...] \fB;\fR
Run
.I command
with its arguments, replacing each
.B {}
in them, including in
.IR command ,
by the file's name as printed.
True if the command exits with status 0.
The
.B ;
is an argument of its own, and the shell needs it quoted.
The first argument that is
.B ;
or
.B +
alone ends the command, so neither can be passed to it.
.IP
The command runs in the current directory of
.BR find ,
sharing its standard input, output, and error.
It is found as
.BR busybox (1)
describes under Running commands, which prefers an applet to a program in
.BR PATH .
If it cannot be started,
.B find
reports that on standard error, the action is false, and the exit status
is not affected.
.TP
\fB\-exec \fIcommand\fR [\fIargument\fR ...] \fB+\fR
Run
.I command
once for many files.
.B {}
must appear exactly once in the whole command, alone or within an argument.
That argument is repeated for each saved file, with
.B {}
replaced by the file's name.
The action saves the file's name and is true.
When the saved names, counting 9 more bytes for each (5 in the x86 kit),
reach 30720 bytes, it runs the command on them and is true if the command
succeeds.
.IP
After the search, the command runs once more on the names still saved, if
any.
If it exits with a nonzero status,
.B find
runs no further
.B "\-exec +"
commands and exits with status 1.
The names saved by an
.B "\-exec +"
inside parentheses are never run after the search.
.B \-quit
discards the names still saved.
Otherwise it is as above.
.TP
\fB\-ok \fIcommand\fR [\fIargument\fR ...] \fB;\fR
Like
.BR "\-exec ;" ,
but first writes the command line to standard error, each argument followed
by a space, then a
.BR ? ,
and reads a line from standard input.
The command runs only if the first character on the line that is not a
space or tab is
.B y
or
.BR Y .
Otherwise the action is false.
Only
.B ;
ends the command.
At end of file the answer is no.
A command that reads standard input competes with the answers.
.TP
.B \-delete
Delete the file or directory.
Always true, even when the deletion fails: the failure is reported on
standard error and the exit status is not affected.
A read-only file is deleted too, and a directory must be empty.
The read-only attribute is cleared first, and stays cleared when the
deletion then fails.
A path named exactly
.B .\&
is not deleted.
Implies
.BR \-depth .
.TP
.B \-quit
Exit at once, without visiting any more files.
The exit status is 1 only if a path before the current one had an error:
errors below the current path are not counted.
.SH OPTIONS
These options, if any, come before the paths.
They may be grouped, as in
.BR \-HL ,
and
.B \-\-
ends them.
There are no long options.
.TP
.B \-L
Follow all symbolic links.
A link counts as what it points to, and a link to a directory is entered.
A link whose target does not exist counts as the link itself, so
.B "\-type l"
matches only such links.
With both
.B \-H
and
.BR \-L ,
.B \-L
applies.
.TP
.B \-H
Follow symbolic links named as paths, but no others.
.TP
.B \-P
Ignored.
Not following symbolic links is the default, and
.B \-P
does not cancel
.B \-H
or
.BR \-L .
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a usage message on standard output and exit with status 0.
It is recognized only as the first argument.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
busybox expands wildcards in its arguments when it starts, even quoted
ones, which serves commands typed in
.BR cmd.exe .
There,
.B \-name \(dq*.c\(dq
becomes
.B \-name
followed by the matching names in the current directory, which gives
wrong results or an invalid expression, so set it to
.B 0
first.
When it is unset, busybox sets it to
.B 0
for the programs it starts, so the kit's shell is not affected.
See
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B BB_OVERRIDE_APPLETS
Applets that
.B \-exec
and
.B \-ok
should not run by name; see
.BR busybox (1).
.TP
.B BB_UMASK
Its group and other write bits are cleared from the permissions that
.B \-perm
tests; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.B PATH
Where
.B \-exec
and
.B \-ok
look for commands that are not applets.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A path or an entry below one could not be examined, a directory could not be
opened, the expression or an option was invalid, a file named by
.B \-newer
or
.B \-samefile
could not be examined, or a command run by
.B "\-exec +"
after the search failed.
See
.B \-quit
for an exception.
.PP
An unrecognized argument is reported with the usage on standard error:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
find: unrecognized: \fIargument\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
A file that cannot be examined or opened is reported as
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
find: \fIname\fR: \fIreason\fR
.EE
.RE
.PP
and the search goes on.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the C source files below the current directory:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find . \-name \(aq*.c\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the source and header files in
.IR src ,
quoting the parentheses and the patterns:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find src \e( \-name \(aq*.c\(aq \-o \-name \(aq*.h\(aq \e)
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the regular files below the current directory, skipping
.I .git
directories:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find . \-name .git \-prune \-o \-type f \-print
.EE
.RE
.PP
Search the users' directories for pictures, ignoring case:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find c:/Users \-iname \(aq*.png\(aq
.EE
.RE
.PP
List files larger than 1 MiB, without crossing to another volume:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find c:/ \-xdev \-type f \-size +1048576c
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the source files that mention
.BR main ,
running
.B grep
on many files at once:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find src \-name \(aq*.c\(aq \-exec grep \-l main {} +
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove log files, passing the names safely to
.BR xargs :
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find . \-name \(aq*.log\(aq \-print0 | xargs \-0 rm
.EE
.RE
.PP
Remove object files directly:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find build \-name \(aq*.o\(aq \-delete
.EE
.RE
.PP
Ask before removing each backup file:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find . \-name \(aq*.bak\(aq \-ok rm {} \e;
.EE
.RE
.PP
Find the empty directories modified in the last hour:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ find . \-type d \-empty \-mmin \-60
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Paths may use
.B /
or
.BR \e ,
but in the shell a backslash quotes the next character, so prefer
.BR / .
The names
.B find
prints begin with the path as given, and only
.B /
is added, so
.B "find \(aqsrc\esub\(aq"
prints names such as
.BR src\esub/a.c .
Paths such as
.IR c:/dir
and
.I //server/share/dir
work.
A bare drive,
.BR c: ,
is the current directory of that drive, and its entries are named
.BR c:name .
A file whose full path, including the current directory when the path is
relative, reaches about 260 characters is reported as not found.
.PP
Matching is bytewise.
Names are UTF-8 on Windows 10 version 1903 or later, so
.B ?\&
and a regular expression's
.B .\&
match one byte, and a non-ASCII character takes two or more of them.
Case folding by
.B \-iname
and
.B \-ipath
is ASCII only.
.BR \-name ,
.BR \-path ,
and
.B \-regex
are case-sensitive, though NTFS is not.
A backslash in a pattern quotes the next character and is not a path
separator, so write
.B /
in a
.B \-path
pattern.
.PP
.B find
treats a symbolic link, a junction, a volume mount point, and an app
execution alias alike, as a symbolic link of type
.BR l .
Type
.B c
matches only the Unix device names that busybox provides, such as
.IR /dev/null .
Types
.BR b ,
.BR p ,
and
.B s
never match.
With
.BR \-L ,
a junction that points to its own parent is entered again and again until
the path is too long.
.PP
Owners and permissions are synthesized; see
.BR busybox (1).
The owner, the group, and others each get read permission, write
permission except for a read-only regular file, and execute permission as
.B \-executable
says.
Then
.BR BB_UMASK ,
002 by default, may clear write permission for the group and others.
So a file you own usually has mode 664, a read-only file 444, and an
executable file or a directory 775.
A symbolic link has mode 777, and a device 666.
A file owned by someone else, when you have full access to it and busybox
is not elevated, gets the owner's permissions for others too, so a
directory such as
.I c:/Users/name
may have mode 777.
When busybox cannot read a file's security information, others get no
permissions.
There are no setuid, setgid, or sticky bits.
The tests
.B \-user
and
.B \-group
are not built in.
.PP
Times come from the file system.
.B \-ctime
and
.B \-cmin
test the time of creation, not of the last change of status.
.PP
The index tested by
.B \-inum
and
.B \-samefile
is the file index of the volume, and
.B \-xdev
compares volume serial numbers.
.B \-links
counts the hard links to a file, and is always 2 for a directory, and for
a junction or other link to a directory.
.PP
To decide whether a regular file is executable, busybox reads the first
bytes of each one whose name ends in neither an executable suffix nor
.BR .dll ,
so a search over many files is slower than a plain listing.
.PP
When standard output is not a console, the names written by
.B \-print
wait in a buffer until it fills or
.B find
exits, and are not flushed before
.B \-exec
runs a command, so the output of commands can come first.
.B \-ok
flushes before it asks.
.PP
Windows has its own
.B find.exe
in
.IR System32 ,
a tool that searches text.
In
.BR cmd.exe ,
make the kit's
.I bin
directory come first in
.BR PATH ,
or run
.BR "busybox find" .
.PP
Differences from GNU
.BR find :
.IP \(bu 2
Only
.BR \-H ,
.BR \-L ,
and
.B \-P
are options, and they cannot follow a path.
.IP \(bu 2
There is no
.B ,
operator, and no
.BR \-mount ,
.BR \-iregex ,
.BR \-newerXY ,
.BR \-anewer ,
.BR \-cnewer ,
.BR \-printf ,
.BR \-ls ,
.BR \-fprint ,
.BR \-readable ,
.BR \-writable ,
.BR \-true ,
.BR \-false ,
.BR \-daystart ,
or
.BR \-regextype .
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-size
does not round up, and accepts only the units
.BR c ,
.BR w ,
.BR b ,
and
.BR k .
.IP \(bu 2
.B "\-exec +"
accepts
.B {}
anywhere in the command, once, and
.B \-ok
does not accept
.BR + .
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-quit
does not run the commands saved by
.BR "\-exec +" .
.IP \(bu 2
The global options are not tests that are always true; see
.BR "Global options" .
.IP \(bu 2
.B \-P
does not cancel an earlier
.B \-H
or
.BR \-L .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR du (1),
.BR grep (1),
.BR ls (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR xargs (1)
